  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Data explored:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` has about 14,000 requests from roughly 1,000 client IPs across 448 URLs. Responses are 200 (12,832), 404 (801), and 503 (441). There are no other 5xx codes. The timestamps run from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, which is after today's date (2026-10-12).\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-23e5ff79 Web logs overview\" (`eff-23e5ff79-web-logs-overview-9965a82e988f`) has six panels:\n- Total requests (metric)\n- Average response bytes (metric)\n- Unique client IPs (metric)\n- Requests over time by response code (bar)\n- Response code breakdown (pie)\n- Top URLs by requests (table, with average bytes)\n\nI set its time range to 2026-10-01 through 2026-12-31 so the data shows up. The default \"last 7 days\" would have shown nothing.\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-23e5ff79 5xx spike\" (id `593eed50-e3e7-46ba-8f39-e84cf6d7aa96`) is an Elasticsearch query rule:\n- Runs every 1 minute.\n- Counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5??` over a 5-minute window.\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Status is `ok` and it is enabled.\n\nTwo things to be aware of:\n- Because the sample timestamps are in the future relative to today, the rule's trailing 5-minute window will probably find no 5xx hits and won't fire until new data arrives. Its \"ok\" status only means it has run without error.\n- I added the tag `EFF-23e5ff79` to the rule. That's my addition, not something you asked for.",
